# Drafted by Gemini (side quests batch 2: fieldtrip), edited.

~ start
finneas_trout [weary]: Welcome to the schoolhouse. I am Finneas Trout. Teaching these children history without giving them nightmares is rather difficult in Wickermoor.
finneas_trout [neutral]: Help me gather five small lessons on the Founder: Pip Oakum's button, a fact from Old Rusty, a bell-rope thread, and a church chalk mark.
finneas_trout [sly]: Come back once you have those four, and I will explain the fifth.
* We will gather them -> accept
* Not now -> END

~ accept
finneas_trout [smile]: Thank you, truly. It is a comfort to have sensible strangers lending a hand.
quest field_trip asked
set field_trip_asked
-> END

~ pip_button
pip_oakum [smile]: A Founder's button for Mr. Trout? That is old coat brass, mister, and it costs a true story from before you came here.
* Tell him a story -> pip_story
* [Performance DC 10] Tell it with flourishes -> pip_story | pip_flat
* Leave -> END

~ pip_story
pip_oakum [smile]: Now that is a proper tale, with teeth in it. Here is your brass button, polished up bright on my sleeve.
set field_pip
set field_items += 1
-> END

~ pip_flat
pip_oakum [weary]: You muddled the middle and went squeaky at the end. Take the button anyway, before Mr. Trout frets himself grey.
set field_pip
set field_items += 1
-> END

~ rusty_fact
old_rusty [sly]: A lesson for the little ones? The Founder was mortal terrified of Tuesdays and bolted his shutters tight, though nobody living recalls why.
set field_rusty
set field_items += 1
-> END

~ rope
Narrator: The clocktower bell-rope hangs frayed and heavy with tallow. Solomon Roderick stands nearby, wiping stone dust from his knuckles.
* [Athletics DC 10] Climb for a thread -> rope_ok | rope_fail
* Ask Solomon for a thread -> rope_ok

~ rope_ok
solomon_roderick [neutral]: One hemp strand for the schoolhouse? Help yourself, friend. The bell will toll the hours all the same.
set field_rope
set field_items += 1
-> END

~ rope_fail
Narrator: Your boot slips on slick timber, leaving you with a scraped knee and empty hands. Just ask Solomon for a thread.
-> END

~ chalk
Narrator: Low on the damp church stones, someone has sketched a white chalk hare with branching antlers.
* Copy the mark -> chalk_ok

~ chalk_ok
Narrator: You trace the horned creature onto a slate scrap, catching gritty lime dust on your fingertips.
set field_chalk
set field_items += 1
-> END

~ report
finneas_trout [weary]: You brought all four. Now for the fifth thing, the only one that truly matters.
finneas_trout [afraid]: Tell the class the Oak of Many Faces is just an ordinary tree. They are too young for what this village worships.
* Tell the pupils the oak is only a tree -> lie
* Tell them the truth, that it is not -> truth
* Let Finneas decide -> teacher

~ lie
finneas_trout [smile]: Thank you for sparing them a little longer. Let them believe in simple wood and leaves while they still can.
set field_outcome = "lie"
quest field_trip done
gold +20
standing newcomers +1
-> END

~ truth
Narrator: The schoolroom falls quiet save for a ticking slate pencil. Then a small girl asks why the bark is still thirsty.
finneas_trout [sad]: Perhaps you are right, and soft words will not keep them safe in a place like this. Here is your pay.
set field_outcome = "truth"
quest field_trip done
gold +10
standing old_ways -1
-> END

~ teacher
Narrator: Finneas looks toward the crooked branches outside and says nothing. The children understand the silence better than words.
finneas_trout [weary]: They already know, do they not? Take this coin for your trouble, and thank you for not making a fool of me.
set field_outcome = "silence"
quest field_trip done
gold +15
-> END
